PHZ Phase 2 — Custom Types, Collections and Object Movement

Phase 2 extends the PHZ object model with inheritance, typed collections and object movement.

Unlike a physics engine, PHZ still does not simulate continuous movement or collisions.

Instead, PHZ modifies ownership, membership, and attributes using ordinary LogTScript property blocks.

---

Overview

New features introduced in Phase 2:

- custom PHZ types
- inheritance
- multiple typed collections
- object movement between containers
- object removal from containers
- collection iteration using "each"
- motor "distance" and "laps"

These additions allow higher level physical systems such as conveyors, elevators, robots and warehouses to be described without introducing loops or scripting constructs.

---

Custom Types

New PHZ types may inherit from existing types.

Syntax:

phz +[car < cont]:
:

General form:

phz +[NewType < BaseType]:
:

Examples:

phz +[wheel < obj]:
:

phz +[robot < cont]:
:

phz +[warehouse < cont]:
:

Current built-in hierarchy:

obj
├── gen
└── cont

Every user defined PHZ type ultimately derives from "obj".

---

Multiple Collections

Containers are no longer limited to a single collection.

Additional typed collections may be declared.

Example:

phz +[car < cont]:

    inside: person[5]

    wheels: wheel[4]

    trunk: obj[100]

:

Collection syntax:

collectionName : Type[max]

where

- "Type" is any PHZ type
- "max" is the maximum number of elements

Examples:

robots: robot[20]

products: obj[1000]

tools: tool[16]

The default collection inherited from "cont" is "inside".

A derived container may redefine it:

inside: person[4]

instead of the generic

inside: obj[16]

---

Typed Collections

Only compatible object types may be inserted into a typed collection.

Example:

wheels: wheel[4]

accepts

wheel

but rejects

scanner

Type checking occurs when objects are moved or spawned.

---

Object Movement

Objects are moved using ordinary property blocks.

There are no dedicated movement statements.

Example:

.conveyor1:{

    move = .box1

    to = .conveyor2

    set = 1

}

When executed,

1. the object is removed from the current collection
2. it is inserted into the destination collection

The object identity remains unchanged.

---

Moving to Custom Collections

Destination may reference any collection.

Example:

.robot:{

    move = .wheel1

    to = .car1:wheels

    set = 1

}

or

.personLoader:{

    move = .person1

    to = .car1:inside

    set = 1

}

---

Moving Between Floors

Objects may change floor without changing ownership.

Example:

.elevator:{

    move = .box1

    toFloor = 2

    set = 1

}

The object remains inside the same container.

Only its "floor" attribute changes.

Typical use cases:

- elevators
- vertical conveyors
- cranes
- storage systems

---

Removing Objects

Removing an object only removes the ownership relation.

Example:

.room:{

    remove = .box1

    set = 1

}

After execution,

.box1

still exists.

It simply no longer belongs to that container.

Removal is not object destruction.

---

Collection Iteration

PHZ intentionally does not introduce

- for
- while
- foreach

Instead, collections expose a temporary iteration placeholder.

each

"each" exists only while a property block is executing.

Example:

.conveyor1:{

    move = :inside:each

    to = .conveyor2

    set = EQ(:inside:each:type,"truck")

}

Meaning:

For every object inside the current container,

if

type == "truck"

move that object into

.conveyor2

The iteration itself is performed internally by the PHZ engine.

No explicit loop exists in the language.

---

Modifying Objects During Iteration

Attributes may also be modified.

Example:

.conveyor1:{

    :inside:each:km =
        ADD(:inside:each:km,\1)

    set = EQ(:inside:each:type,"truck")

}

Only matching objects are modified.

---

Relative Collections

Inside a property block,

:inside

always refers to the current container.

Example:

.conveyor1:{

    move = :inside:each

    to = .warehouse

    set = 1

}

is equivalent to

.conveyor1:inside

Outside the block, the full object path is used.

---

Motor Distance

Motors expose two movement counters.

value

Current shaft position.

Used for servo positioning.

distance

Distance travelled since the previous revolution.

Example:

0

1

2

...

99

100

0

1

When the maximum value is reached,

distance wraps back to zero.

---

Motor Laps

Every time "distance" wraps,

"laps" increases.

Example:

distance

0 → 100 → 0

results in

laps

0 → 1

This makes conveyors and rotary systems easy to synchronize.

---

Conveyor Example

comp [motor] .motor:
    maxDistance: 100
:

phz [conveyor] .conv1:
    motor: .motor
:

.conv1:{

    move = :inside:each

    to = .conv2

    set = EQ(.motor:distance,100)

}

Whenever the conveyor completes one travel distance,

all matching objects may be transferred.

---

Elevator Example

comp [servo] .servo:
:

phz [elevator] .lift:
    motor: .servo
:

.lift:{

    move = :inside:each

    toFloor = 2

    set = EQ(.servo:value,100)

}

The elevator does not physically animate objects.

Instead, their "floor" attribute changes when the servo reaches its destination.

---

Design Philosophy

Movement in PHZ is logical rather than physical.

A move operation changes relationships.

It does not simulate trajectories.

Likewise,

typed collections describe ownership,

not geometry.

The PHZ engine determines how iteration is performed.

Scripts only describe what should happen,

never how to iterate through objects.

This keeps PHZ deterministic, declarative, and fully compatible with the LogTScript execution model.